// Chapter 7, "The Restore." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-16):
// ISACA Certification Exam Candidate Guide, version 1.26 (2026), Appendix A, CISA Examination Content Outline (effective August
//   2024): topics 4B1 business impact analysis; 4B2 system and operational resilience; 4B3 data backup, storage, and restoration;
//   4B4 business continuity plan; 4B5 disaster recovery plans. Quiz items 16 (3A4, a system enforced preparer block) and 17 (3B1,
//   the business owner signs UAT) are spaced review of chapter 5:
//   https://www.isaca.org/-/media/files/isacadp/project/isaca/certification/exam-candidate-guides/2026/exam-candidate-guide.pdf
//   https://www.isaca.org/credentialing/cisa/cisa-exam-content-outline
// NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems (maximum tolerable downtime set by the
//   owner; RTO must normally be shorter than the MTD because reprocessing time is added; RPO is the point in time data can be
//   recovered to and isn't part of the MTD; cold, warm, hot, mobile, and mirrored sites, mirrored the dearest, cold the cheapest
//   and slowest; reciprocal agreements; offsite storage of backups; backups tested regularly to make sure files can be retrieved;
//   tabletop exercises are discussion based and deploy no equipment; functional exercises run up to full scale):
//   https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-34r1.pdf
// NIST SP 800-84, Guide to Test, Training, and Exercise Programs for IT Plans and Capabilities ("test" is reserved for testing
//   systems or components; exercises validate plans): https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-84.pdf
// Business continuity planning (BIA identifies the effects of disruption and sets recovery priorities; RTO and RPO; tabletop,
//   medium, and complex exercises; issues found in testing go back to analysis): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_continuity_planning
// Business continuity and disaster recovery auditing (tests are functional or discussion based; plans must be tested, not
//   unpracticed documents): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_continuity_and_disaster_recovery_auditing
// Backup (3-2-1 rule: three copies, two media, one offsite; offline storage largely immune to online failures; an online replica
//   has limited value as a backup; checksum validation): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backup ; incremental backup (changes since
//   the preceding backup): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incremental_backup ; differential backup (all changes since the last
//   full; restore needs the full and the last differential): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_backup ; grandfather,
//   father, son rotation: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backup_rotation_scheme
// Backup site (cold, warm, hot; distance from the primary site): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backup_site ; single point of
//   failure: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_point_of_failure ; high availability (eliminate single points of failure,
//   redundancy, reliable crossover): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_availability ; IT disaster recovery (RTO, RPO, DR tests):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IT_disaster_recovery
// Dates: Saturday, March 20 to Monday, March 22, 2027. US daylight saving started March 14 and the UK changes March 28, so Denver is
//   UTC minus 6 and six hours behind London: 2 PM in Denver is 8 PM in London. Weekdays computed: Friday, March 5 (event); Monday,
//   March 8 (BCP invoked); Sunday, March 14 (settlement processing resumed); Thursday, November 19, 2026 (DR test); Tuesday,
//   May 14, 2024 (BIA approved). The remediation window ran Saturday 10 PM to Sunday 4 AM (ch6).
// Figures (drill, re-derived): claim business interruption $182,000,000 for 9 days, 182 / 9 = $20.22M a day. BIA impact of a full
//   day's outage $6,800,000 (2.9 + 3.1 + 0.8); nine days at that rate 9 x 6.8 = $61.2M; difference 182 minus 61.2 = $120.8M;
//   ratio 20.22 / 6.8 = 2.97. Nine days counted as 216 hours against an RTO of 4 hours: 216 / 4 = 54 times. Backup report 2026:
//   365 nights (2026 isn't a leap year), restore test PASS 365 of 365, all stamped the minute the backup ended. BCP call tree 22
//   names, 9 gone. DR plan recovery team 7 names, 2 still at Meridian.
// Facts set here for later chapters: BIA approved Tuesday, May 14, 2024 by the CIO, prepared by IT with Pellridge, no sign off from
//   the Head of Settlement Operations; settlement release MTD 24 hours, RTO 4 hours, RPO 15 minutes, $6.8M a day. Backups: nightly
//   full 1:00 AM, logs every 15 minutes; "restore test" PASS stamped at backup end, no restore job in the scheduler; Leonard's 2026
//   report sampled 25 and called it effective. Weekly offline copy discontinued June 2023 ("replaced by replication"). March 5:
//   production database, the synchronous replica at Pellridge's secondary site, and the backup server's disk copies all encrypted;
//   recovery from Pellridge's settlement file archive (the ch4 archive, not in Meridian's data inventory), its first ever restore,
//   procedure written during recovery; settlement processing resumed Sunday, March 14. DR plan version 3, last revised October
//   2018, covers the legacy system; Pellridge recovery site added by memo November 2019, procedures "to follow". DR test Thursday,
//   November 19, 2026: walkthrough, nothing failed over or restored, "RTO 4 hours: achievable", PASSED, signed by Pellridge's
//   service manager, acknowledged by the CIO. BCP version 2 (2020), owner CIO, never tested, 9 of 22 on the call tree gone, fallback
//   "settlement deferred until systems restored", held only on the corporate file share; invoked Monday, March 8, 9:05 AM, after
//   a printed copy turned up in the CFO's office; client banks told Monday, 11:30 AM. Renewal proposal form (December 2026):
//   quarterly restore tests Yes; an offline copy Yes; last DR test November 19, 2026, passed; time to restore settlement 4 hours.
//   Change completed Sunday 2:51 AM; release 2 replaced the settings tables at 11:20 PM. Three states from ch6: sealed copy taken
//   9:12 to 9:48 PM Saturday by a firm both counsels agreed on (unnamed), restored once to an isolated machine Sunday 6:05 AM,
//   hashes match; or logs only (settings overwritten, scope limitation sent Sunday); or nothing kept (settings and the release
//   service's own logs gone, wider scope limitation). Maisie has been timing Laura's defense talk (47 minutes against 45; Laura
//   cuts the London slide and puts it back); Henry texts Laura to keep it. Lou's inventory (labels only): tea tins dated when
//   opened, three green cozies, a shelf of red tabbed folders with examination numbers (no count given), one dated 1994 with no
//   number and empty, napkins in a rubber band; Lou bagged the shelf; the MC folder's W/P 1 tab and W/P 17 under it (ch1) read as
//   placed, not proved; the top napkin says "Colour", corrected.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 7, part: `Part Two: The Dome`, title: `The Restore`, weeks: `Saturday, March 20 to Monday, March 22`,
  tasks: [`4B1`,`4B2`,`4B3`,`4B4`,`4B5`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `The waitress came at five past nine on Saturday with two mugs and put one down on each side of the booth by the window. Neither went upside down. Henry got coffee. Maisie wanted tea, so the waitress went back for the small metal pot and a box of bags, and held the box out until Maisie had picked one.`,
    `He'd fetched her at eight, as promised. Laura answered the door half in her coat, laptop under her arm. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Noon,"</span> she said. Then, <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Thank you."</span> On the kitchen table behind her a stopwatch lay face up next to a pile of printed slides. Maisie came out in two sweaters. He'd told her the window booth was cold, and it was.`,
    `At twenty past nine his phone buzzed in his coat on the hook. It went again five minutes later. He didn't get up. Maisie glanced at the coat, then at him, said nothing, and put three sugars in her tea.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"It's terrible,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You were right."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"She was right. I only passed it on."</span>`,
    `She wanted to know what the two of them had talked about, every Saturday for nine months. Work, mostly, he said. Dot would ask what he'd found that week, and then tell him what he'd missed.`,
    `Maisie thought about that. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Ask me, then."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Ask you what?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"What I found this week."</span>`,
    `So he asked. She'd found that Mum's defense talk ran forty seven minutes and was meant to run forty five. She knew because the stopwatch was hers, from track in seventh grade, and Mum had borrowed it on Monday and never given it back, so Maisie had taken over the timing. Every run, Mum got to the slide about London and cut it, and the talk came in at forty one. Then back went the slide, and she was over again.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"It's the best one,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"It's got the river in it. She says someone on her committee thinks it's old news."</span>`,
    `He knew what was on that slide. It was the work Laura had done in London before 2013, the work she'd put down when one of them had to be at home and it was always going to be her. In all the years since he'd never once told her he knew what that had cost. He didn't tell Maisie now.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"She shouldn't cut it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You tell her."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I might."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Mum says might means no."</span> There was no edge in it at all. She said it as a fact about the world, like water boiling sooner in Denver.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Then yes."</span>`,
    `She nodded, as if a line on a list had been ticked, and took a sugar packet from the bowl and tore a strip off the top. Then she stopped, and looked at what she was holding, and at him. She put it down unfinished beside her mug. Neither of them mentioned it.`,
    `They stayed until eleven. He had her home at eight minutes to twelve, and he didn't take the phone out of his coat until her front door had shut.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: getting it back`,
    sections: [
      { h: `How long can you go without it`,
        body: [`Every company I ever examined told me all its systems were critical, Lime Street. If everything's critical, nothing is, and you can't plan for nothing. A business impact analysis is how a company decides what matters most and how soon it must have it back. It comes first. You can't choose a recovery site, buy a backup system, or write a plan until you know what you're recovering, and by when.`,
          `It goes process by process. For each one: what does the company lose if this stops for an hour, a day, a week? Money, and also customers, contracts, fines, and whatever the regulator says on Monday. The damage grows with time, and seldom in a straight line. Out of that come three numbers. The maximum tolerable downtime is the longest the process can stop before the damage can't be undone. The recovery time objective is how fast the systems behind it must be back, and it has to sit well inside the maximum, because once the systems are up somebody still has to catch up on the work. The recovery point objective is how much data the business can afford to lose, counted backward from the failure. Fifteen minutes means the company can lose fifteen minutes of transactions and not one more.`,
          `Those numbers decide the spending. The shorter the recovery time, the dearer the recovery: four hours needs systems standing ready somewhere, and four days needs a contract and a phone number. The shorter the recovery point, the more often data has to be copied.`,
          `Now the part people get wrong. The numbers belong to the business. IT can tell you what each target costs. Only the people who run the process, and the management above them, can say how long they can live without it. A BIA written by IT, approved by IT, and never signed by the owner of the process is IT's opinion of somebody else's business. And it goes stale. New system, new product, new vendor: do it again. I always look at the date on the front, and the signature, and then I ask whether the person who signed could tell me what the numbers mean.`],
        terms: [[`Business impact analysis (BIA)`,`An assessment of each business process: how the impact of disrupting it grows over time, which sets recovery priorities and objectives.`],[`Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)`,`The longest a process can be disrupted before the harm is unacceptable. Set by the business.`],[`Recovery time objective (RTO)`,`How quickly a system must be restored after a disruption. Shorter than the MTD.`],[`Recovery point objective (RPO)`,`How much data, measured in time before the failure, the business can afford to lose. It sets how often data is copied.`]],
        exam: `The BIA comes first and drives continuity and recovery strategy. RTO and RPO are set by business process owners, informed by the BIA. A shorter RTO or RPO costs more. The RTO must fall inside the maximum tolerable downtime. ISACA's glossary calls the longest acceptable outage the acceptable interruption window. Its maximum tolerable outage is something else: how long the business can keep running in alternate mode.` },
      { h: `Built to stay up`,
        body: [`Resilience is a system's ability to keep going when parts of it fail, so that a failure never turns into a disaster at all. You buy it with redundancy. Two power feeds. Two network routes from two carriers. Disks mirrored, so a dead one costs nothing. Servers in a cluster, so when one stops another picks up its work. Load balancing, so no single machine carries everything. A standby site holding a live copy of the data, ready to take over in minutes.`,
          `Then hunt for the single point of failure: the one part, or one person, or one company, whose failure stops everything. It isn't always a box on a diagram. Sometimes it's the only engineer who understands the system, and sometimes it's the vendor who holds every copy. Ask what happens if it goes, and whether anyone has written the answer down.`,
          `Know what each kind of redundancy is for. A mirrored disk protects you from a disk failing. A cluster protects you from a server failing. A second site protects you from losing a building. None of them protects you from bad data. Replication copies whatever the primary holds, as quickly as it can, and it copies a corrupted or encrypted database just as faithfully as a healthy one. That's why a replica isn't a backup. A backup is a copy from a moment you chose, kept somewhere the damage can't follow.`,
          `Resilience lives in operations too: capacity to absorb a surge, monitoring that notices a part failing before it fails, spares, maintenance contracts, and staff who have actually practiced switching over. A company that has never once failed over to its standby doesn't know whether it can.`],
        terms: [[`Redundancy`,`Duplicate components, paths, or sites, so the failure of one doesn't stop the service.`],[`Single point of failure`,`Any one component, person, or supplier whose failure stops the whole system.`],[`Clustering and failover`,`Servers grouped so that if one fails, another takes over its work automatically.`],[`Disk mirroring (RAID 1)`,`Writing the same data to two disks, so one can fail without loss.`],[`Replication`,`Copying data to another system as it changes. It copies errors and corruption too, so it isn't a backup.`]],
        exam: `Redundancy removes single points of failure. Mirroring and clustering protect against component failure, and a second site against site loss. Replication isn't a backup, because it copies corruption along with everything else.` },
      { h: `Copies`,
        body: [`A backup is a copy you can get back. That last part is the whole of it. Plenty of companies have copies. Fewer have backups.`,
          `A full backup copies everything. It's slow to make and quick to restore. An incremental copies only what changed since the last backup of any kind, so it's quick to make, and a restore needs the last full and every incremental since, in order. A differential copies everything changed since the last full, so each one grows through the week, and a restore needs only the last full and the last differential. The exam likes to ask which copies you'd need on a Thursday. Draw the week on paper. It's easier.`,
          `How often you copy comes from the recovery point objective. A day's RPO can live on a nightly backup. Fifteen minutes means copying the transaction logs all day long. How long you keep each copy is the rotation scheme's job. The old grandfather, father, son pattern keeps dailies, weeklies, and monthlies, so you can reach back past a problem nobody noticed for a while. A rule I like: three copies of anything that matters, on two different kinds of storage, one of them away from the building. I'd add that at least one should be offline, or locked so nothing on the network can change it, because whatever can reach production can usually reach the backup server sitting next to it.`,
          `Offsite means far enough away that the same fire or flood or power cut can't reach both, close enough that you can get the copies back fast, and guarded as well as the original. A backup holds everything production holds, so it gets the same access control, and these days encryption, with the keys kept somewhere you'll still be able to reach after the disaster.`,
          `Then the test. A backup job that says it finished has told you it wrote something. It hasn't told you anything can be read back. The only proof is a restore: put a copy back on a machine and check it against the source with counts and totals, as you would a conversion. Do it on a schedule, for every critical system, and write down how long it took, because that's your real recovery time. When somebody shows me a column of restore tests that all passed, I look at the times. A restore that finishes in the same minute as its backup didn't happen.`],
        terms: [[`Full, incremental, differential`,`Everything; changes since the last backup of any kind; changes since the last full backup.`],[`Rotation scheme`,`The rule for how long each backup is kept and when media are reused, such as grandfather, father, son.`],[`Offsite and offline copies`,`Copies kept far enough away to survive a local disaster, and out of reach of the production network.`],[`Restore test`,`Actually recovering data from a backup and checking it against the source. The only proof a backup works.`]],
        exam: `Restore testing is the only proof that backups work. Backup frequency follows the RPO. An incremental restore needs the last full plus every incremental since. A differential restore needs the last full plus the last differential. Keep at least one copy offsite and out of reach of the production network.` },
      { h: `The plan for the business`,
        body: [`A business continuity plan is how the business keeps going while its systems are down, and how it gets back to normal. It's wider than IT. It covers people, buildings, suppliers, customers, and the regulators who'll want a phone call, and it rests on the BIA: the most critical processes first, in the order the analysis set.`,
          `A decent plan says who may invoke it and on what trigger, who's on the team and how to reach them, what the business does by hand or somewhere else while the systems are out, who tells customers and regulators and when, and how to come back. Somebody senior in the business owns it, and senior management approves it. IT owns one piece, the disaster recovery plan for the systems, and that piece serves the business plan, not the other way round.`,
          `Then keep it alive. Every reorganization, every new system, every resignation changes it. A call tree full of people who've left is a plan nobody has read. Test it, train the people in it, and fix what the test finds. And keep copies where the disaster can't reach them. A plan stored only on the file server it's meant to work without is a plan you'll read after the emergency, if at all. Paper, in a few homes and a few cars, is unfashionable, and it works.`,
          `When I audit one, I read it, and then I go and ask three people named in it what they'd do on the day. Their faces tell me more than the binder does.`],
        terms: [[`Business continuity plan (BCP)`,`The business's plan for keeping critical processes running during a disruption and returning to normal. Owned by the business.`],[`Invocation`,`The formal decision, by a named person on a defined trigger, to put the plan into action.`],[`Call tree`,`The list of who contacts whom, and how, when the plan is invoked.`],[`Alternate procedures`,`Manual or off site ways of doing critical work while systems are unavailable.`]],
        exam: `The BCP is owned by the business, approved by senior management, and built on the BIA. It must be kept current, tested, and stored where it can be reached during a disaster, including offsite. After a test, the most important thing is fixing what it found.` },
      { h: `Getting the machines back`,
        body: [`The disaster recovery plan is the technical half: how IT gets systems and data back within the objectives the business set. It names a recovery team and their alternates, the order systems come back in, procedures written so that someone other than the author could follow them, and the place everything comes back to.`,
          `That place is the recovery site. A hot site is equipped and ready, with current data, and can be running in hours. A warm site has some of the equipment and needs data restored and work done first. A cold site is space, power, and cabling, and takes days or weeks. A mirrored site runs a live copy of everything and costs the most. There are mobile sites on trucks, and reciprocal agreements to borrow another company's spare capacity, which look cheap until you notice both companies have grown and neither has room. The choice follows the RTO and the money.`,
          `Then testing, which is where plans meet the truth. From least to most: a checklist review, where people check their parts of the plan are current. A walkthrough, or tabletop, where the team sits round a table and talks through a scenario. A simulation, where they act it out, with surprises, without touching production. A parallel test, where the recovery systems are actually brought up and run real work alongside production, so the results can be compared. And a full interruption test, where production is switched off and the business runs on the recovery site. That one proves the most and risks the most, and it's rarely the place to start.`,
          `A test needs objectives set before it starts, measured against the RTO and RPO, and a written result: what came back, how long it took, what failed, and who's fixing it by when. Be very careful what you let a discussion prove. A tabletop can show that people know their roles. It can't show a single system will come back in four hours, because nobody brought one back. If the report says otherwise, ask for the timings, and then ask who took them.`],
        terms: [[`Disaster recovery plan (DRP)`,`IT's plan for restoring systems and data within the RTO and RPO after a disaster.`],[`Hot, warm, cold site`,`Recovery sites by readiness: ready in hours; partly equipped; space and power only.`],[`Tabletop or walkthrough`,`A discussion of the plan against a scenario. Nothing is recovered.`],[`Parallel test`,`Recovery systems are brought up and process real work alongside production, which keeps running.`],[`Full interruption test`,`Production is shut down and operations move to the recovery site. The most thorough and the riskiest test.`]],
        exam: `A hot site recovers fastest; a cold site is cheapest and slowest. A parallel test gives strong assurance without stopping production; a full interruption test is the most thorough and the riskiest. A walkthrough or tabletop can't demonstrate recovery times. Measure test results against objectives and fix the gaps.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f7s1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B1`, title:`Home by noon`, q:`With Meridian's continuity file open, where should Henry start?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const c6=((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{}),sealed=c6[2]===2,logs=!sealed&&c6[9]===2;
        return [`He read them in the car outside Laura's house with the engine off. Teresa Vreeland never said hello in a text either.`,`<prop type="text" from="Teresa Vreeland" time="9:20 AM">Meridian's continuity file came in overnight. Business impact analysis, DR plan, last DR test, a year of backup reports, the business continuity plan, the full restoration report, and their workings for the business interruption figure. In your inbox.</prop>`,
        (sealed
          ?`<prop type="text" from="Teresa Vreeland" time="9:25 AM">Meridian agrees to the copy. A firm both of us can live with takes it tonight, before the window opens. You won't be there. I've told them so.</prop>`
          :logs
          ?`<prop type="text" from="Teresa Vreeland" time="9:25 AM">Meridian confirms the logs are being kept. Nothing yet on the release service itself.</prop>`
          :`<prop type="text" from="Teresa Vreeland" time="9:25 AM">I sent the preservation request at five yesterday. Meridian's general counsel is out until Monday. So, I'm told, is everyone else.</prop>`),
        `He drove back down to Denver with the file unopened, which took some discipline, and opened it on Wazee Street at ten to one. Seven documents. The claim said nine days without settlement had cost Meridian $182 million. Every one of the seven said something about how long Meridian had believed it could be down, and how fast it could come back.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask Halverson how long a recovery like this ought to take.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Halverson is Meridian's firm, and it ran the recovery. Its answer would be an opinion about its own client's nine days, given in the middle of a claim.`,
          why:`An opinion from a firm paid by the claimant isn't independent evidence. Start from the organization's own records of what it planned.`},
        {t:`Start with the business impact analysis before the other six.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The impact analysis goes on top, and he reads all eleven pages before he opens anything else. The recovery plan's four hours, the backup schedule, the continuity plan's trigger, even the claim's daily loss: each of them should trace back to a line in it. Where one doesn't, that's a finding. Where the BIA itself is wrong, everything built on it is wrong in the same direction.`,
          why:`The business impact analysis comes first. It identifies the critical processes and sets the recovery objectives that the continuity plan, the recovery plan, and the backup strategy all have to meet.`},
        {t:`Start with the business interruption workings behind $182 million.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The workings are thirty pages of Meridian's finance team multiplying things. By the end of them he knows how Meridian got to $182 million, and nothing at all about whether Meridian could ever have come back in less than nine days.`,
          why:`The claim's estimate is the claimant's case. Test it against what the business decided, before the loss, it could tolerate and recover from, which is the impact analysis.`},
        {t:`Start with the DR plan and its four hour recovery target.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The plan promises recovery in four hours. Four hours according to what? He has to go and find the document the number came from, and it's the one he skipped.`,
          why:`The recovery plan is built to meet targets set elsewhere. Read the impact analysis first, so you know what the plan was supposed to achieve.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B1`, title:`Six point eight`, q:`The impact analysis puts a figure on one day's outage. What should Henry do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The page that mattered in the impact analysis was a table, and the row that mattered was the first.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian business impact analysis, approved Tuesday, May 14, 2024 (extract)">Process: settlement release. Criticality: 1 of 5.<br>Maximum tolerable downtime: 24 hours. Recovery time objective: 4 hours. Recovery point objective: 15 minutes.<br>Estimated impact of a full day's outage: $6,800,000 (lost fees $2.9M; penalties to client banks $3.1M; other $0.8M).<br>Prepared by: IT, with Pellridge. Approved: CIO. Process owner sign off (Head of Settlement Operations): none.</prop>`,`He laid the claim summary beside it, the page he'd marked up on the first morning. Business interruption, nine days: $182,000,000.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Record who made each daily figure and the basis behind it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Two columns. The BIA, May 2024: $6.8 million a day, prepared by IT and Pellridge, approved by the CIO, never signed by the business that would lose the money, made when nobody had a reason to want it bigger. The claim, March 2027: $20.2 million a day, made by finance after the loss. Neither is the truth yet. Then a line under both: the four hour recovery target was set by IT too, and the only person who could say what four hours meant to settlement never put a name to it. He asks counsel for the basis of every line in each.`,
          why:`A BIA should be prepared with the business process owners and approved by them and senior management. When a pre loss estimate and a post loss claim disagree, record both, their sources and dates, and test the basis of each.`},
        {t:`Recompute the claim at the BIA's rate: nine days, $6.8 million each.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It comes to $61.2 million, and it's his number now, built on a table that the settlement business never signed. He's adjusted a claim with another estimate.`,
          why:`The BIA is evidence of what Meridian expected, not a measure of what it lost. Substituting one estimate for another isn't testing either, and the adjustment isn't the investigator's call.`},
        {t:`Prefer the claim's figure to a BIA prepared in calm weather.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The BIA was made in calm weather. It's also the only figure Meridian wrote down before it had anything to gain, and he's just set it aside for the one it wrote afterward.`,
          why:`A contemporaneous estimate made before the loss is relevant evidence. Dismissing it in favor of the claimant's later figure adopts the claim without testing it.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian's finance team to explain why the two figures differ.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The request goes off through counsel. The answer will come from the people who built the larger number. Meanwhile, who made the smaller one, and when, is printed at the bottom of it.`,
          why:`Asking is reasonable, but first record what each figure is, who produced it, and when. The source and timing of each estimate is evidence in itself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B3`, title:`The same minute`, q:`Meridian's backup reports show a year of PASS results. What should Henry do with them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The backup reports were a year of the same line, three hundred and sixty five times. He took one night at random, then ten more, and then all of them.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Backup report, settlement database, 2026 (extract)">Nightly full backup, 1:00 AM. Transaction log backups every 15 minutes.<br>Typical night: backup ended 2:47 AM. Restore test: PASS, 2:47 AM.<br>Restore test result, 2026: PASS, 365 of 365. Restore test time equal to backup end time: 365 of 365.<br>Restore test job in the scheduler: none found.</prop>`,`Leonard's 2026 report had tested the same thing. Twenty five nightly backups sampled, each completed, each with restore test PASS. Conclusion: effective.${G.trustedia?` On the ninth he'd let a line like that do his testing for him.`:``}`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Restore a backup himself on a spare machine to see if it works.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He'd need Meridian's backups, Meridian's keys, and a machine, and he'd be performing the insured's control for it, alone, on evidence in a claim.`,
          why:`The auditor evaluates whether the organization tests its restores. Performing the restore personally would mean handling the auditee's data and evidence outside any proper process.`},
        {t:`Compare each restore test's time with when the backup finished.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`Restoring a settlement database takes hours. These finished in the minute the backup did, every night for a year, and the scheduler has no restore job at all. So PASS meant the backup had written something. Nobody ever read one back. Meridian went into March 5 not knowing whether it could get its own data back, and so did Leonard, who'd sampled twenty five from the same column and called it effective.`,
          why:`Backups are only proven by restoring them and checking the result against the source. A "restore test" stamped at the same time the backup ends is a completion flag, and relying on it means recoverability was never tested.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the scheduler logs behind each PASS result.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel asks. The report's own timestamps answer it first, and the scheduler has no restore job to log anything.`,
          why:`Asking for the underlying logs is reasonable. The timing shows no restore was performed, and that's the finding.`},
        {t:`Rely on 365 passes and internal audit's sample of the same field.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He notes backups as tested. Three hundred and sixty five passes, and internal audit's twenty five on top. Every one of them came out of the same column.`,
          why:`A status field isn't a test. Check what the "restore test" actually did before relying on it, and don't treat internal audit's reading of the same field as corroboration.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B3`, title:`Every copy in reach`, q:`Ransomware encrypted the database, its replica and the backup server on March 5. What should Henry make of that?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Pages 61 to 64 of the restoration report were the part of the claim nobody had asked him to read closely, so he read them closely.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Restoration report, settlement environment (Pellridge for Meridian), pages 61 to 64 (extract)">Friday, March 5: production settlement database encrypted. Replica at the secondary site encrypted within the same minutes. Backup server and its disk copies encrypted.<br>Weekly offline copy: discontinued June 2023 ("replaced by replication").<br>Recovery source: Pellridge settlement file archive. First restore from this archive. Procedure written during recovery.<br>Settlement processing resumed: Sunday, March 14.</prop>`,`The archive was the one he'd found in Pellridge's invoices at $8,400 a month, the copy Meridian's own data inventory didn't know it had.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask Halverson for its account of how the backups were reached.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel adds it to the list. How is a question for the forensic people, and Halverson works for Meridian. Why nothing was left out of reach is in the documents on his desk.`,
          why:`The forensic account may be useful, though the firm isn't independent. The control finding, no offline copy, stands without it.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian buy locked backup storage before its renewal.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It might be good advice. It's a fix for a finding he hasn't written, offered to the insured in the middle of its claim.`,
          why:`Record the condition and its effect first. Remediation is management's decision, and advising the auditee during the claim isn't the investigator's role.`},
        {t:`Record the backups as lost to the attack and move to the restore.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes "backups compromised in the attack." It's true. It leaves out that every copy was somewhere one event could reach, by design, since June 2023.`,
          why:`Whatever reached the backups, the design left no copy out of reach. Where and how copies are stored is a control, and its failure belongs in the finding.`},
        {t:`Check where each copy was kept relative to the production network.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Three copies, and all of them in reach. The replica copied the damage, which is what replicas do. The backup server sat on the same network as production. The one copy kept offline was stopped in June 2023 with a note saying replication had replaced it, which replaced a backup with a mirror. What saved the company was a copy no plan of Meridian's listed, restored for the first time, with the procedure written as it went. Most of nine days are in that sentence.`,
          why:`At least one backup should be kept offline or immutable, and offsite, out of reach of anything that can reach production. Replication isn't a backup. A recovery source that has never been restored is untested, and recovery from it will be slow.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B2`, title:`Ten o'clock`, q:`The DR plan's resilience design replicates the database to a second site. What should Henry do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He was home by seven, in the flat where three boxes from April still stood against the kitchen wall, labeled in his own hand. He'd told Maisie yes. He sat down and wrote it before the yes could go stale.`,`<prop type="text" from="Henry" time="7:12 PM">Maisie says you cut the London slide every time and put it back. Keep it in. Find the two minutes somewhere else. I remember that work.</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Laura" time="7:16 PM">She told you about the river.</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Laura" time="7:31 PM">I'll think about it.</prop>`,`It was more than he'd expected, and he left it there.`,`At ten, across town, Pellridge's engineers would be starting on the release service. ${(((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{})[2]===3)?`He'd offered to be there, on Thursday, and Teresa had explained slowly why not.`:`Nobody had asked him to be there, and he'd made sure nobody would.`} He made tea in a pot that kept no heat, and opened the resilience design from the back of the DR plan.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement platform, resilience design (Pellridge, 2019, extract)">Primary: Meridian data center. Secondary: Pellridge facility.<br>Settlement database replicated synchronously to the secondary. Automatic failover within 5 minutes. Designed availability: 99.95 percent.<br>Recovery from data corruption: restore from backup (see backup standard).</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Weigh it against corrupted data, not only against a lost site.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The design answers the question somebody asked in 2019: what if a server dies, or a building goes dark. A synchronous replica is a perfect copy of everything, at once, mistakes included. Against bad data the design had one line, pointing at the backups whose restore tests passed in the minute they ended. The BIA's four hours were priced on the first kind of failure. March 5 was the second.`,
          why:`Resilience measures such as replication and failover protect availability against component and site failures. They don't protect against data corruption, which needs tested, isolated backups. The recovery objective has to be met for the events that matter.`},
        {t:`Conclude whoever did this knew the replica would follow.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's a theory about somebody's knowledge, and replicas follow by design. Anybody would have got the same result.`,
          why:`A design weakness isn't evidence of intent. Report the control gap and leave the theory out.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel why Meridian didn't fail over to the secondary site.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel will ask, and the page in front of him has the answer. There was nothing clean to fail over to.`,
          why:`The question is reasonable, but the design answers it. The finding is that the design relied on replication for something replication can't do.`},
        {t:`Count two sites, synchronous copies and failover as strong resilience.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It is, against the things it was built for. On March 5 it failed over nothing, because both sites held the same encrypted database within minutes.`,
          why:`Redundancy protects against component or site failure. Judge a resilience design against the events it has to survive, including corrupted or encrypted data.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s6`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B3`, title:`What was kept`, q:`Meridian offers to restore the release service's old settings from Pellridge's backups. How should Henry respond?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const c6=((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{}),sealed=c6[2]===2,logs=!sealed&&c6[9]===2;
        return [...(sealed
          ?[`Teresa's email was waiting when he woke on Sunday, sent at 6:52, with a one page note attached. A firm both counsels had agreed on had come to the release service at nine on Saturday night and gone before ten, before the window opened.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Note of preservation, release service (forensic firm agreed by both counsels)">Saturday, March 20, 9:12 PM to 9:48 PM: complete copy of the release service's configuration, settings tables, and logs. Hashes recorded at capture.<br>Sunday, March 21, 6:05 AM: copy restored once to an isolated machine. Hashes match.<br>Held under seal. Hash list held by both counsels.</prop>`,`He stopped at the second line and stayed there a while. Somebody had made a copy, and before telling anyone it was safe, had got it back out to make sure. It held the settings table as it stood on Saturday night. With no change history, that was the nearest anyone could now get to March 5, and it was a great deal nearer than nothing.`]
          :logs
          ?[`Teresa's email was waiting when he woke on Sunday. Meridian had answered Friday's 4:58 request at 11:40 on Saturday night, with the change under way: release 2 had replaced the release service's settings tables at 11:20 PM, as planned, and no copy had been taken first. The logs he'd asked for on Friday morning had been kept, copied to a sealed drive and sent to Teresa. The threshold and the approver list as they had stood on March 5 were gone.`,`Before breakfast he wrote the limitation and sent it to Giles and Teresa. The release service's settings for March 2 to 5 were overwritten by the insured's change on March 20, after a written request to preserve them. The report can't say what the threshold or the approver list were on those dates. The logs for the period are held.`]
          :[`Teresa's email was waiting when he woke on Sunday, and it was short. Release 2 had replaced the release service's settings tables at 11:20 PM. The service's own logs had been cleared when the new version started, as the upgrade was designed to do. Nobody had copied either first. Friday's request was sitting in an inbox nobody would open till Monday.`,`He wrote the limitation before breakfast, and it was a wide one. The settings for March 2 to 5: gone. The service's own record of the night of February 26, and of every release from then to March 5: gone. What remained was the deployment tool's log and DOME, both Meridian's, already disagreeing. He sent it to Giles and Teresa with the dates, and noted in his own file that the request had gone a day later than it should have, and whose job it had been to make it.`]),
        `At 8:15 she forwarded Meridian's own account of the night.`,`<prop type="email" from="Teresa Vreeland, forwarding Meridian's general counsel" to="Henry Ashby" subject="Weekend change" time="8:15 AM">The change completed at 2:51 AM on Sunday. Release 2 controls are in production. Pellridge's nightly backups of the release service, taken under Meridian's backup standard, hold its configuration as it stood before the change and can be restored should the insurer require it.</prop>`,`Under Meridian's backup standard. He had a year of what that standard called a restore test on the desk beside him.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept Pellridge's backups as the record of the old settings.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He notes that the configuration is recoverable from backup. Nothing anywhere shows that a backup made under that standard has ever been read back.`,
          why:`A backup is evidence of recoverability only when a restore has been shown to work. This standard's restore tests never restored anything.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian to restore the backup and send him the old settings.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`A restore is the right test. But Meridian would run it, on its own systems, and send him whatever came out, with nobody independent there to see what went in.`,
          why:`Only a restore proves a backup, so asking for one is sound. Evidence for a claim should be restored under independent control, with integrity checks, not by the claimant alone.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge's engineers to write down what the settings were.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The people who administered the table would describe it from memory, a fortnight on. It would read well, and it would be their word.`,
          why:`Recollection by the people who controlled the settings is the weakest possible evidence of what the settings were. Look for a record, restored and verified independently.`},
        {t:`Treat the backups as unproven until an independent restore.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Meridian's sentence goes into the file as Meridian's, with the time and the signature. Under it go the facts that bear on it: the backups were made under a standard whose restore tests pass in the minute the backup ends, so nothing shows any of them can be read back. If the insurer ever needs one, it'll be restored by someone Meridian doesn't control, with hashes, and both counsels told. Then, on its own line, what he actually holds of the release service as it stood before Saturday, and what his report can't say without the rest.`,
          why:`An untested backup is a claim about recoverability, not evidence of it. Where evidence must be recovered, the restore should be done under independent control with integrity checks, and the auditor states plainly what was preserved and what the report can't cover.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s7`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B5`, title:`Passed`, q:`What should Henry make of November's DR test report?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The November test report was two pages, and the second page was signatures.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Disaster recovery test report, settlement platform, Thursday, November 19, 2026">Test type: walkthrough. Scenario: loss of the primary data center.<br>Participants: Pellridge service manager, three Pellridge engineers, Meridian CIO.<br>Systems failed over: none. Data restored: none.<br>Recovery time objective, 4 hours: achievable.<br>Result: PASSED. Signed: Pellridge service manager. Acknowledged: CIO.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask Meridian to run a full DR test this week to settle it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`He'd be directing a payments company to switch off production in the middle of its own claim, to answer a question about last November that the report answers on its first page.`,
          why:`Testing is management's decision, and a test now can't show what capability existed before the event. Evaluate the evidence of past testing.`},
        {t:`Accept the signed November test as proof of four hour recovery.`, s:0, flag:`acceptedrestore`, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`He writes "DR tested November 2026, passed." It sits in his file as a fact. Nothing was failed over and nothing was restored, and his file doesn't say so.`,
          why:`A test report is only as good as what the test did. Accepting "passed" without evidence that anything was recovered relies on a discussion as proof of capability.`},
        {t:`Check what the November test actually failed over or restored.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`A walkthrough is people round a table talking through the plan. Nobody failed anything over, and nobody restored a byte. "Achievable" was an opinion, written by the vendor who'd have to achieve it, and marked passed. Four months later the real recovery took nine days. The report isn't false. It's a discussion with a test's name on it, and everyone who read it afterward was told the four hours had been tested.`,
          why:`Tabletop or walkthrough exercises check roles and the plan's content. Only tests that actually bring systems up at the recovery site, such as parallel or full interruption tests, can show recovery within the RTO. Results should be measured against objectives.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the attendance list and minutes of the test.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`They'll show who sat in the room. The report says what the room did, which was talk.`,
          why:`More detail is reasonable to request. The report shows the test type and that nothing was recovered, so the finding can be made now.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s8`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B5`, title:`Written for the old one`, q:`Meridian's DR plan was last revised in October 2018. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The plan itself came after lunch. He'd left it till then because the test had told him what to expect, and he wanted to be wrong.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement disaster recovery plan, version 3 (extract)">Last revised: October 2018.<br>Systems in scope: legacy settlement system; legacy release gateway.<br>Recovery team: 7 named (2 still employed by Meridian).<br>Recovery site: Pellridge facility, added by memo November 2019. Procedures: "to follow."<br>Settlement file archive: not mentioned.</prop>`,(G.skippedpir
        ?`On Thursday he'd written "PIR not required (sponsor)" under Longitude and gone on to the next thing. A review in 2020 would have asked what the new platform did to the plan for getting it back. He found the line in his file and reopened it.`
        :(((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{})[4]===2)
        ?`On Thursday he'd listed what a review in early 2020 would have walked into. He added this to the list. A new platform goes live, and nobody asks what it does to the plan for getting it back.`
        :`On Thursday Longitude's missing review had stayed an open question in his file. Here was part of the answer. A new platform went live, and nobody asked what it did to the plan for getting it back.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask the CIO, through counsel, when the plan was last reviewed.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel sends it. The plan's own front page says October 2018, and the answer will either agree with it or be a story.`,
          why:`Asking is fine, but the document shows it hasn't been revised since before the current system existed.`},
        {t:`Check which system the plan's procedures would actually recover.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The plan brings back the legacy settlement system, which was switched off on a Sunday night in September 2019. Two of its seven named people still work at Meridian. The recovery site was added by a memo that promised procedures and never delivered them, and the archive that actually saved the company isn't in it at all. So on March 5 Meridian had no plan for recovering the system it was running.`,
          why:`DR plans must be updated whenever systems change and reviewed regularly, with current procedures, teams, and recovery sources. A plan for a retired system gives no assurance about the live one.`},
        {t:`Redraft the recovery steps for Longitude to show the gap.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He'd be writing Meridian's recovery plan for it, as the insurer's investigator, and then judging a claim about how well it recovered.`,
          why:`Writing the auditee's plan is a management function and impairs independence. Report that the plan is out of date.`},
        {t:`Accept the plan on its named recovery site and recovery team.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`The site and the team exist on paper. The plan recovers a system nobody has used since 2019, with a team that mostly left.`,
          why:`A DR plan has to match the systems actually in production and be kept current. Existence isn't adequacy.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s9`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B2`, title:`One door`, q:`The continuity file shows who runs each recovery option. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By six on Sunday evening he was at the continuity file's last section, the one that said who ran each way Meridian could have got its settlement service back.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Routes back to service, from the continuity file">Secondary site: Pellridge. Settlement file archive: Pellridge. Restore procedure: written by Pellridge, during the event.<br>Release service hosting and its backups: Pellridge. Last DR test: run and signed by Pellridge.<br>Pellridge contract, 2019: availability 99.95 percent. Recovery commitments, incident terms, continuity plan: none.</prop>`,`At 6:12 his phone buzzed.`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="6:12 PM">she ran it twice today. i didnt time it. she asked me not to</prop>`,`He read it as good news and wasn't sure it was.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rely on Pellridge's contracted availability of 99.95 percent.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The number covers whether the service is up in an ordinary month. It says nothing about getting it back after an event, and the contract has no terms for that at all.`,
          why:`An availability target isn't a recovery commitment. Check what the vendor is bound to do in a disaster, and whether it has been tested.`},
        {t:`Conclude Pellridge set things up so Meridian could never leave.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Maybe. It could as easily be seven years of cost cutting and nobody asking. He has the dependency on paper and nothing at all about why.`,
          why:`A dependency is a finding about risk. Motive needs evidence of its own, and speculation weakens the report.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel whether Pellridge has a continuity plan of its own.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a good question, and Meridian should have asked it in 2019. The bigger finding is on the page in front of him.`,
          why:`A critical vendor's own continuity arrangements matter. The core finding is that every recovery route depends on that one vendor.`},
        {t:`Mark which firm Meridian relied on for every way it could recover.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He puts it on the whiteboard under the shopping list, one line for each way back and a name beside each. A single point of failure doesn't have to be a box. Here it's a company. The standby site, the archive, the backups of the release service, the people who wrote the restore as they went, and the people who marked the last test passed are all Pellridge, under a contract with no recovery terms. If Pellridge failed, or wouldn't help, Meridian had no route back at all. And it's the vendor whose own release on February 26 still has no ticket.`,
          why:`Resilience design should remove single points of failure, including dependence on a single supplier for every recovery path. Critical vendors need contractual recovery commitments that the customer tests.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s10`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B4`, title:`Call tree`, q:`Meridian's continuity plan for settlement operations is owned and approved by the CIO. What should Henry take from it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Monday he was at the desk by half past six, with the bicycle shop's shutters still down, and the business continuity plan was the first thing he opened. The DR plan was IT's. This one was supposed to be the business's.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian business continuity plan, settlement operations, version 2 (2020, extract)">Owner: CIO. Approved: CIO.<br>Invocation: outage of settlement processing over 4 hours.<br>Call tree: 22 names (9 no longer employed).<br>Alternate procedures: "Client banks to be advised. Settlement deferred until systems restored."<br>Tests: none recorded. Training: none recorded.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ring a few names on the call tree to see who still picks up.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He'd be cold calling the insured's staff, some of them former staff, in the middle of a claim, about the night of the event.`,
          why:`Contact with the auditee's people goes through the agreed channel. The plan's own records show it's out of date.`},
        {t:`Check whether the business ever signed or tested the plan.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The business continuity plan for settlement belongs to the CIO, who also approved it. Nobody from settlement operations signed it. It has never been tested, nobody has been trained in it, and nine of the twenty two people it tells to ring each other have left. For the one process the BIA said could stand a day's outage at most, the plan's alternate procedure is to tell the banks and wait. So the BIA said a day, and the plan's only answer was to stop.`,
          why:`A BCP should be owned by the business, approved by senior management, kept current with staff and system changes, tested, and trained. Alternate procedures must let critical processes continue within the maximum tolerable downtime.`},
        {t:`Accept the written plan with its owner and its call tree.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It has all three. Nine of the twenty two people on the tree have gone, and the plan's answer to a stopped settlement system is to wait for it.`,
          why:`A plan has to be owned by the business, kept current, and tested. Its existence alone isn't evidence it would work.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel who has been trained on the plan and when.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It goes on the list. Then he notices he's asking Meridian to tell him something its plan wrote down in two words, and crosses it off.`,
          why:`Training matters, and asking is fine. The plan's own record shows no training or testing, and the ownership problem needs no answer from anyone.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s11`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B4`, title:`Where the plan was`, q:`The incident log shows what happened to the continuity plan on March 5. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The incident log had come in Thursday's batch, under the job record he'd read for the hold at 4:52. He'd read it for the times. Now he read it for the plan.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian incident log, settlement outage (extract)">Friday, March 5, 4:40 AM: incident declared.<br>Business continuity plan: not invoked. Plan held on corporate file share (unavailable).<br>Monday, March 8, 9:05 AM: BCP invoked. Printed copy located in the CFO's office.<br>Monday, March 8, 11:30 AM: client banks notified.</prop>`,`At 12:40 his phone buzzed.`,`<prop type="text" from="Lou" time="12:40 PM">Done. Labels only, like I said. I'll bring it by at six.</prop>`,`He wrote <b>I'll be here.</b>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Check where copies of the plan were kept and when it was used.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The plan lived on the file share it was meant to work without, and the share was encrypted with everything else. Nobody kept a copy off the network, so nobody invoked it until a printout surfaced in the CFO's office on Monday morning. The banks were told at half past eleven that day, more than three days after the event. Whatever the outage cost per day, the first three days of it ran without the plan.`,
          why:`Copies of the BCP must be kept where they're available when systems and buildings aren't: offsite, on paper, with key people. A plan that can't be reached can't be invoked on time.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel why the client banks weren't told until Monday.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a fair question, and Teresa sends it. The log gives the answer two lines up.`,
          why:`The notification delay matters. The cause is in the record: nobody could reach the plan.`},
        {t:`Tell Giles the outage, the delay and the cost are Meridian's fault.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's a coverage conclusion dressed as a finding, and coverage isn't his to decide. He'd be handing Giles an opinion where Giles needs facts.`,
          why:`Report the control failure and its effect accurately to the appropriate party. Conclusions about liability or coverage belong to those responsible for them.`},
        {t:`Count the plan as invoked once a printed copy turned up on Monday.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It was invoked. A copy turned up three days into a nine day outage, and the banks heard on Monday morning.`,
          why:`A continuity plan has to be available during the disruption it's for. The delay is a control failure, not bad luck.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s12`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4B5`, title:`The form`, q:`Meridian's December renewal form answers questions on backups, offline copies and DR tests. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At two, which was eight in the evening in London, he opened the underwriting file. When Meridian renewed its cover in December, it had answered the underwriters' questions about recovery, months before it had any reason to want the answers read closely.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Renewal proposal form, December 2026, recovery section (extract)">Are backups of critical systems tested by restoring them at least quarterly? Yes.<br>Is at least one backup copy kept offline, out of reach of the production network? Yes.<br>Date and result of last disaster recovery test: November 19, 2026, passed.<br>Time to restore critical settlement systems: 4 hours.</prop>`,`His father had read forms like this for forty years. He'd said once that the answers were the cheapest part of any risk, because the people who wrote them never had to pay for them.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave the renewal form to underwriting as a coverage matter.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Coverage is underwriting's. Telling the people whose job it is what he's found is his, and he's just left out the most significant fact of the day.`,
          why:`Leaving out a significant fact the decision makers need distorts the report. Report it, and let them decide what it means.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian's CIO to explain each answer in writing first.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The explanations may matter later. Sent now, the request tells the insured what he's found before the people he reports to have seen it.`,
          why:`Management's explanation has its place, but the facts should go to the appropriate party first, and the request would signal the finding to the auditee.`},
        {t:`Tell Giles the false form gives grounds to refuse the claim.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He'd be giving a coverage decision to the man whose decision it is, as if it were his. Whether any answer matters to the policy is for Giles and counsel to say.`,
          why:`The investigator reports facts and findings. Whether a misstatement affects cover is a legal and underwriting decision for the appropriate people.`},
        {t:`Set each answer beside the evidence and send it to Giles.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`Four rows, four columns: what the form said, what the evidence shows, its source, its date. Restore tests quarterly: none ever performed; PASS was stamped as each backup ended. An offline copy: discontinued June 2023. Last DR test passed: a walkthrough, nothing recovered. Four hours: nine days. He adds no word about what it means for the policy. That's for Giles and counsel. It goes to Giles at 2:40 with Teresa copied, complete.`,
          why:`Recovery capability is shown only by tests that actually restore systems against the RTO. When the evidence contradicts what was represented, report the facts completely and promptly to the appropriate party, and leave decisions outside your remit to them.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f7s2`, title:`Nine days, two prices`,
     setup:[`The claim puts business interruption at $182,000,000 for an outage of nine days. Meridian's own business impact analysis, approved in May 2024, estimated a full day's outage of settlement release at $6,800,000, with a recovery time objective of 4 hours. Treat the nine days as 216 hours.`],
     fields:[{label:`Claimed loss per day of outage, in $ millions (two decimals)`,answer:20.22,tol:0.01},{label:`Nine days at the BIA's daily figure, in $ millions (one decimal)`,answer:61.2,tol:0.05},{label:`Difference between the claim and that figure, in $ millions (one decimal)`,answer:120.8,tol:0.05},{label:`Actual outage as a multiple of the RTO`,answer:54,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Per day claimed = $182,000,000 ÷ 9 = $20,222,222, or $20.22 million a day.`,`Nine days at the BIA's figure = 9 × $6,800,000 = $61,200,000, or $61.2 million.`,`Difference = $182.0 million minus $61.2 million = $120.8 million. The claim runs at about 2.97 times the BIA's daily figure. That doesn't make either number right. The BIA was made before the loss by IT and the vendor, without the business owner; the claim was made after it by finance. Both go in the file, with who made them and when, and the basis of each gets tested.`,`216 hours ÷ 4 hours = 54. The outage ran 54 times the recovery objective, and 9 times the 24 hour maximum tolerable downtime. An objective nobody ever tested tells you what somebody hoped.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f7s7`, title:`Which test was it?`, intro:`Dot's notebook had a page headed "What did they actually do?" Sort these by the kind of test they describe.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Team leads sit in a conference room and talk through what each of them would do if the data center flooded overnight.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:0, why:`Discussion only, nothing touched. A tabletop, or walkthrough.`},
      {prompt:`The recovery team role-plays a scripted outage step by step while a facilitator adds surprises. Nothing is moved to the recovery site and production isn't touched.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:1, why:`They act it out, with surprises, and recover nothing. A simulation.`},
      {prompt:`Recovery systems are brought up at the alternate site and process the same day's real transactions while production keeps running, and the outputs are compared.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:2, why:`Real work on the recovery systems, alongside production. A parallel test.`},
      {prompt:`Production is shut down on a Sunday and the business runs from the recovery site until Tuesday.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:3, why:`Production off, recovery site live. A full interruption test, the most thorough and the riskiest.`},
      {prompt:`Meridian's November test: the vendor's staff and the CIO read through the plan. Nothing was failed over and nothing restored.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:0, why:`A walkthrough, whatever the report called its result. It can't show a recovery time was met.`},
      {prompt:`The one test that can cause the very outage it's meant to prepare for.`, choices:[`Tabletop`,`Simulation`,`Parallel`,`Full interruption`], answer:3, why:`Switching production off is the point of a full interruption test, and the risk of it.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY purpose of a business impact analysis?`, opts:[`To select the most cost effective recovery site`,`To list the threats likely to cause an outage`,`To rank processes by the impact of losing them`,`To confirm backups can be restored in time`], a:2, why:`A BIA identifies the critical processes and how the impact of disrupting each grows over time, which sets recovery priorities and objectives. Site selection and backup testing come later and rest on it.`, task:`4B1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Who should set the recovery time objective for a settlement process?`, opts:[`Its owner, using the BIA`,`The IT manager for its servers`,`The vendor that hosts it`,`The IS auditor reviewing it`], a:0, why:`How long a process can be down is a business decision, made by the process owner from the impact analysis. IT works out how to meet it, and the auditor evaluates it.`, task:`4B1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A company shortens a system's recovery time objective from 24 hours to 2 hours. What is the MOST likely effect?`, opts:[`Less data will be lost in an outage`,`The BIA no longer needs updating`,`Backups can be taken less often`,`Recovery will cost more to provide`], a:3, why:`A shorter RTO needs recovery resources standing ready, such as a hot site or standby systems, which costs more. How much data can be lost is the RPO, a separate measure.`, task:`4B1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Why is real time replication to a second site NOT a substitute for backups?`, opts:[`Replication is slower than backup`,`It copies corrupted data as well`,`Replicas can't be encrypted`,`The second site costs more to run`], a:1, why:`Replication copies whatever the primary holds, damage included, almost at once. A backup is a copy from a chosen point in time, kept where later damage can't reach it.`, task:`4B2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A critical application runs on one server with no standby. Which control would BEST improve its availability?`, opts:[`Backing it up twice each night`,`A faster processor in the server`,`A cluster with automatic failover`,`A longer vendor support contract`], a:2, why:`Clustering with failover removes the single point of failure, so another server takes the work if one stops. Backups help recovery, not availability.`, task:`4B2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`What does disk mirroring MAINLY protect against?`, opts:[`A user deleting data in error`,`The failure of one disk drive`,`A fire in the computer room`,`Malware that encrypts files`], a:1, why:`Mirroring writes the same data to two disks, so one can fail without loss. It copies a deletion or encryption to both disks at once, and a fire takes both.`, task:`4B2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which provides the BEST evidence that backups can be used to recover a system?`, opts:[`Test restores, checked to source`,`Backup job logs showing no errors`,`A vendor statement on backup quality`,`Backup media stored in a fire safe`], a:0, why:`Only a restore shows that a backup can be read back complete and correct. Job logs show only that something was written.`, task:`4B3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A full backup runs Sunday night and a differential backup runs each night from Monday to Saturday. The system fails on Thursday morning. What is needed to restore it?`, opts:[`Every backup taken since Sunday`,`Wednesday's differential alone`,`Sunday's full and Monday's differential`,`Sunday's full and Wednesday's differential`], a:3, why:`A differential holds every change since the last full backup, so the restore needs the full and only the most recent differential. With incrementals, you'd need the full and every incremental since.`, task:`4B3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A system's recovery point objective is 15 minutes. What does this MOST directly determine?`, opts:[`How quickly the system must be restored`,`Which recovery site should be used`,`How often its data must be copied`,`How long backups must be kept`], a:2, why:`The RPO is how much data, measured in time, the business can afford to lose, so copies must be taken at least that often. How fast the system comes back is the RTO.`, task:`4B3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Who should own an organization's business continuity plan?`, opts:[`Senior business management`,`The IT operations manager`,`The internal audit function`,`The disaster recovery vendor`], a:0, why:`Continuity is about keeping the business running, so business management owns the plan and approves it. IT owns the disaster recovery part, and audit evaluates the whole.`, task:`4B4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Where should copies of the business continuity plan be kept?`, opts:[`On the main file server`,`With the IT department only`,`In the document system`,`Offsite, in paper form too`], a:3, why:`The plan has to be usable when the systems and building it describes aren't. Copies kept offsite, including on paper and with key people, can be found during the disaster.`, task:`4B4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A business continuity plan's call tree lists several people who left two years ago. What does this MOST likely indicate?`, opts:[`The plan was written too early`,`The plan isn't kept current`,`The plan has too many contacts`,`The plan should be outsourced`], a:1, why:`Departed staff on the call tree show the plan hasn't been reviewed after organizational changes, so other parts are likely out of date too.`, task:`4B4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which disaster recovery test gives strong assurance without interrupting production?`, opts:[`A checklist review`,`A tabletop walkthrough`,`A parallel test`,`A full interruption test`], a:2, why:`A parallel test brings the recovery systems up and runs real work on them while production carries on. Checklists and tabletops recover nothing, and a full interruption test stops production.`, task:`4B5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which type of recovery site can usually resume critical processing the FASTEST?`, opts:[`A hot site`,`A warm site`,`A cold site`,`A reciprocal agreement`], a:0, why:`A hot site is equipped and configured, with current data, and can run within hours. A warm site needs setup and data restored, a cold site needs equipment installed, and a reciprocal agreement depends on another company's spare capacity.`, task:`4B5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A disaster recovery test report is marked "passed," but no systems were recovered during the test. What should the IS auditor conclude?`, opts:[`The plan meets its objectives`,`Recovery wasn't demonstrated`,`The test was too disruptive`,`The RTO should be lengthened`], a:1, why:`A test that recovered nothing can't show the systems would come back within the RTO. A discussion tests roles and the plan's content, not recovery itself.`, task:`4B5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which control BEST prevents the person who prepared a payment file from also approving it?`, opts:[`A written desk procedure`,`A monthly supervisor review`,`Training on the policy`,`A block the system enforces`], a:3, why:`A control the system enforces can't be skipped, while procedures, training, and later reviews depend on people remembering. Review of chapter 5.`, task:`3A4`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`Who should sign off user acceptance testing before a system goes live?`, opts:[`The vendor's project manager`,`The lead developer`,`The business owner`,`The IT operations team`], a:2, why:`UAT is the business confirming the system meets its requirements, so the business owner signs, before go live. A vendor signing for its own work isn't acceptance. Review of chapter 5.`, task:`3B1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{},ar=!!(s.flags.acceptedrestore||G.acceptedrestore),c6=((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{}),sealed=c6[2]===2,logs=!sealed&&c6[9]===2,heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2,p10=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[0],lifted=!!G.liftedpage||p10===0;
    return [
    ...(ar
      ?[`One line in his own file still said what he'd written on Sunday: <b>DR tested November 2026, passed.</b> Beside the proposal form it couldn't stand. He wrote to Giles that he'd accepted the test on Sunday on the report's word, that he'd been wrong to, and why, and filed the note next to the line rather than over it.`]
      :[]),
    (sealed
      ?`At half past five Teresa sent the firm's receipt for the sealed copy, with both counsels' signatures on the hash list and nobody else's.`
      :logs
      ?`At half past five Teresa sent Meridian's reply to the scope limitation. It disagreed with every word except the dates.`
      :`At half past five Teresa sent Meridian's reply to the scope limitation. It said the change had been approved by the board in the ordinary course, which was true, and was the problem.`),
    `Lou came up the stairs at five past six with two coffees, which meant he'd decided something about the visit before he got there. He put one in front of Henry, and a single printed page beside it, and kept his coat on.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Labels. Like I said. I'm not giving you her life. I'm giving you what's written on the outside of the boxes."</span>`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Denver Police Department, property inventory, residence of D. Kessler, Monday, March 22 (extract, labels only)">Kitchen: tea, loose, 4 tins, each labeled with the date opened. Tea cozies, hand knitted, green, 3.<br>Study, shelf 2: folders, manila, red tab, each tab marked with an examination number. Count and contents to be logged by the property unit.<br>Same shelf, far end: 1 folder, red tab, marked "1994." No examination number. Empty.<br>Desk, top drawer: paper napkins, diner, a stack in a rubber band. Handwriting, green ink.</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"I bagged the shelf. All of it. It's in our property room, and that's where it stays till somebody with a reason signs for it."</span> He drank some of his coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"She dated her tea, Ashby. And she's got a folder with nothing in it and a year on the front. You know what I hate."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Guessing."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"So don't make me. 1994. That mean anything to you?"</span>`,
    ...(heard
      ?[`It meant a voice on the line from Muswell Hill on the first Tuesday. The Denver risk. Ninety four. It meant a slip in Vellacott's archive that he'd asked Priyanka to leave where it was. None of it was anything he could put in front of a detective and call a fact. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's what her top sheet said. Evidence, see 1994. It's been on my whiteboard since the twelfth."</span>`,`All of that was true. Lou looked at the whiteboard, and then at him, for a little longer than he needed to.`]
      :[`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's what her top sheet said. Evidence, see 1994. It's been on my whiteboard since the twelfth, and I still don't know what it is."</span>`,`Lou looked at the whiteboard as if the year might own up.`]),
    `Henry thought about the folder on the table at the Brown Palace, and the corner of the second sheet. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"The folder she had at the hotel. The tab said W/P 1. The page right under the top sheet said W/P 17. Not two. Seventeen."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Meaning."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Meaning she may have put it there, where I'd see it first. Or it slipped. I'd like to know which."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Everything from that hotel room's in our property room, folder and all. Top sheet's photographed. Nobody's turned a page since, because nobody's given me a reason I can write down."</span>`,
    ...(lifted
      ?[`<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Slipped a quarter inch. Then you made it an inch."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Before I touched it. A quarter."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"So you say."</span>`]
      :p10===1
      ?[`<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Your page is in my photograph. Quarter inch. I looked."</span>`]
      :p10===2
      ?[`<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"It's in your photograph too. Quarter inch. Doesn't tell me why."</span>`]
      :[]),
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I'd like someone to turn that page."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Then you know how. In writing, through your lawyer, to me. And I'll tell you now, nobody opens that bag because a man has a feeling about page numbers."</span> He stood, and picked up his coffee, and put it down again. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Put it in anyway."</span>`,
    `At the door he stopped with one hand on the frame. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"The napkins. I read the top one. Then I put the band back."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"What did it say?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"One word. Colour."</span> He spelled it, with the U. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"She'd crossed the U out."</span> He went down the stairs before Henry could find anything to say to that.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch07.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`Night Shift`
});
